  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Resilient &amp; Green-Based Business for Inclusive Access to Healthy Diets</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Despite its potential to enhance smallholder farmers&apos; resilience to climate change and resilient food systems, however, the low uptake and adoption of Control and Self Assessment( CSA )practices and technologies in Ethiopia prevent them from reaching their full potential. The following are the main reasons for the low adoption rate of implementation of CSA practices and technologies specifically for the smallholder and the marginalized people. 1) Technical constraints include energy output from solar panels being low in areas with cloud coverage, and repair of solar panels and solar systems is difficult. 2) Financial Constraints include higher upfront costs for farmers compared to other pumps, Lack of credit facilities, and environment for farmers, developers, and suppliers. 3) Market constraints include limited availability of suitable or customized products (sizes, types of pumps); lack of market information, lack of modest quality affordable products, Lack of maintenance and repair services ecosystem, Lack of spare parts, and Low farm density. 4) Institutional constraints include restrictive monetary; lack of institutions to train and build capacity of local entrepreneurs, service providers, and technicians no demonstrations by either state or market; and lack of coordination among policies, institutions, and agencies. 5) cultural: Women are significantly more constrained to access information, credit, and services.    Objectives of the project: (i) To expand and scale up geographic coverage for sales of innovative solar -powered irrigation technology for an improved healthy diet; (ii) To expand and scale up for supply of organic seeds/seedlings provision service with innovative embedded services including provision of vermicomposting for climate -smart agricultural practices; (iii) To achieve a minimum of 20% growth in revenue.  The project will be implemented through partnership business model between Solar Village Ethiopia (SVE) and Oxfam in Ethiopia as project</narrative>
  </description>
